      title: `mark>Landmark Clinical Trials on Coronary Prophylaxis</mark>`,
      content: `The clinical efficacy of pre-procedural prophylactic revascularization was rigorously evaluated by major randomized controlled trials, radically shaping current practice guidelines: 

- **The Hertzer Cleveland Clinic Series (1000 Patients):** Landmark consecutive angiographic screening revealed that 60% of peripheral vascular patients had advanced or severe coronary stenosis (>70%). Among patients without any clinical suspicion of heart disease, more than one-third still exhibited severe, correctable or inoperable coronary lesions. Preoperative coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) was associated with an inherent surgical mortality of 5.5% in vascular patients—significantly higher than the 1% to 2% mortality seen in isolated CABG cohorts. While 5-year survival appeared improved by prior CABG (12% vs. 26% mortality), the initial hazard reduced its net benefit. 

- **The Coronary Artery Revascularization Prophylaxis (CARP) Trial:** This multi-center trial randomized 1190 patients with significant coronary stenosis to either prophylactic revascularization (via CABG or PCI) or optimized medical management prior to elective open aortic or infrainguinal vascular surgery. Prophylactic 

revascularization failed to reduce long-term mortality at 2.7 years (22% in the revascularization arm vs. 23% in the medical arm). Immediate perioperative mortality (3.1% vs. 3.4%) and perioperative myocardial infarction (12% vs. 14%) were statistically indistinguishable between the groups. Note that the CARP data cannot be extrapolated to patients with unstable cardiac symptoms, left main coronary artery disease (≥50% stenosis), severe aortic stenosis, or advanced left ventricular ejection fraction failure, as these high-risk categories were strictly excluded. 

- **The DECREASE-V Trial:** This trial screened 1880 high-risk vascular surgery candidates, randomizing 430 patients with three or more clinical risk factors to undergo noninvasive stress testing. Patients demonstrating extensive stress-induced ischemia (26%) were randomized to pre-procedural revascularization or no revascularization. Angiography confirmed 3-vessel disease in 67% and left main disease in 8% of this cohort. Prophylactic intervention provided no survival or ischemic benefit: the 30-day incidence of all-cause death or nonfatal MI was 43% in the revascularization group versus 33% in the medically optimized group. At 1 year, composite endpoint rates remained equivalent (49% vs. 44%). _Note: This trial has subsequently come under significant scrutiny due to formal findings of scientific misconduct by the principal investigator, but its reported results are central to historical source data._ 

The clear lack of benefit derived from prophylactic mechanical revascularization highlighted an incomplete understanding of perioperative MI. While demand-driven ischemia (supply-demand mismatch) is a predominant mechanism confirmed by angiographic studies, many acute events are atherothrombotic, precipitated by the destabilization and rupture of vulnerable plaques located within vessels lacking critical, flow-limiting stenosis. Consequently, perioperative strategies prioritizing pharmacological plaque stabilization are superior to routine mechanical revascularization.`
