      content: `Postoperative pulmonary complications represent a profound source of morbidity, occurring with maximum frequency following open abdominal aortic or thoracoabdominal procedures. Prominent complications include acute atelectasis, pneumonia, respiratory failure, and severe exacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) driven by historical tobacco abuse. Preoperative arterial blood gas analysis is vital to establish a firm baseline; a baseline partial pressure of arterial carbon dioxide ( **_PaCO₂ > 45_** mm Hg) denotes hypercapnia and strongly predicts heightened postoperative ventilatory failure. 

Optimization includes targeted bronchodilator therapy, though clinicians must carefully monitor for beta-agonistinduced tachyarrhythmias or acute myocardial ischemia. For patients with advanced COPD or reactive reactive airway disease, a brief preoperative course of oral glucocorticoids (prednisone 40 mg/day for 2 days) is highly effective. Active pulmonary infections must be completely eradicated with appropriate antibiotic therapy preoperatively. Intraoperatively and postoperatively, the utilization of thoracic epidural techniques significantly minimizes the requirement for systemic opioids, thereby dampening opioid-induced respiratory depression, while regular incentive spirometry and continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) offer established recovery benefits.`
