      content: `The profound increases in left ventricular preload seen during thoracic or supraceliac clamping are heavily governed by blood volume shifting out of the highly compliant splanchnic venous vasculature. The splanchnic circulation holds roughly 25% of the total circulating blood volume, with nearly two-thirds (>800 mL) capable of rapid autotransfusion. Cross-clamping proximal to the splanchnic system eliminates distal arterial inflow, causing an immediate drop in pressure within splanchnic capacitance vessels. This allows the highly compliant veins to passively recoil, forcing vast quantities of blood proximally into the central venous circulation. Superimposed on this passive mechanism, a massive neuroendocrine surge of plasma epinephrine and norepinephrine triggers active venoconstriction, actively diminishing splanchnic venous capacitance and amplifying venous return to the heart. Conversely, during infrarenal clamping, blood volume below the clamp can safely shift into the intact splanchnic venous segments above the clamp, dampening any sudden rise in preload.`
